###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_76cfad65_2147_44d6_86da_dc89c27c1782
:EMMO_76cfad65_2147_44d6_86da_dc89c27c1782 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_f1025834_0cd2_42a1_bfeb_13bec41c8655 ;
                                           rdfs:seeAlso isbn:9780471736967 ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "PolyCrystallineMaterial"@en ;
                                           :conceptualisation "From a strict thermodynamic and physical standpoint, a polycrystalline material is in a crystalline state. The word \"state\" often implies a single, uniform condition, and a polycrystal is full of messy, mismatched grain boundaries. However, in materials science and condensed matter physics, the \"state\" of a material is defined by its atomic structure and thermodynamic phase, not by its macro-scale uniformity."@en ;
                                           :elucidation "A material composed of a collection of many small individual crystalline units called grains."@en ;
                                           :note """A polyscrystalline state is a mosaic of tiny single crystals. Inside any single grain, the atoms are perfectly ordered. However, each grain is tilted or rotated at a different angle relative to its neighbors. Where these mismatching orientations meet, they form irregular atomic interfaces known as grain boundaries. Most engineered metals, alloys, and ceramics are polycrystalline.
Inside a polycrystal, more than 99% of the atoms sit precisely on a periodic, highly ordered crystalline lattice. They have undergone a distinct phase transition (solidification/crystallization) into a crystalline state. The only atoms not in a perfect lattice are the thin, few-atom-wide walls at the grain boundaries."""@en .


