###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_a5da5097_9fb3_42c2_a6b2_b104f06ff773
:EMMO_a5da5097_9fb3_42c2_a6b2_b104f06ff773 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_66281f4c_ce3b_46e0_bd24_b5cdcce93094 ,
                                                           :EMMO_b655d54f_d812_4635_a1a5_8949ee4383fc ;
                                           skos:example "Borosilicate"@en ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "Glass"@en ;
                                           :conceptualisation "To be considered a true glass, an amorphous ceramic must have been formed via the glass transition (usually by cooling a liquid fast enough to freeze its chaotic structure in place). A glass lacks long-range translational order. Its structure is disordered and isotropic (the same in all directions)."@en ;
                                           :elucidation "An amorphous ceramic material obtained through a glass transition."@en ;
                                           :note """A glass is not always a mechanical solid. From a strict physics and thermodynamics standpoint, glass is a state of matter. Specifically, an amorphous network trapped in a structurally frozen state. Because this state is entirely dependent on temperature, glass can exist as a flowing, viscous liquid just as easily as it can exist as a brittle windowpane.
The boundary between \"liquid glass\" and \"solid glass\" isn't a sharp melting point like you see when ice turns to water. Instead, it is a gradual, temperature-dependent zone called the glass transition temperature Tg."""@en ;
                                           :wikipediaReference <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass> .


