###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c5e9e390_8836_4440_b4fd_c906fdd4f438
:EMMO_c5e9e390_8836_4440_b4fd_c906fdd4f438 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                           rdfs:subClassOf :EMMO_4db521a3_f80f_436b_b9c7_e4b52b1383ea ;
                                           rdfs:isDefinedBy isbn:9780471736967 ;
                                           skos:altLabel "Ceramic"@en ;
                                           skos:example "Concrete"@en ,
                                                        "Glass"@en ,
                                                        "Solid made of Al₂O₃"@en ,
                                                        "Solid made of SiC or Si₃N₄"@en ,
                                                        "Solid made of ZrO₂"@en ;
                                           skos:prefLabel "CeramicMaterial"@en ;
                                           :elucidation "An inorganic non-metallic substance made from elements (metals, non-metals, semi-metals like silicon) combined into compounds (oxides, carbides, nitrides) that are typically hard, strong, brittle, corrosion-resistant, and good insulators."@en ;
                                           :note """A ceramic with a disordered, random structure frozen in place, is considered amorphous. An amorphous ceramic can be categorised according to its formation process, depending on the fact that it went or not through a glass transition in glass or amorphous solid.
The distinction is about the thermodynamic state of the material. Because a true glass was once a liquid, it occupies a specific metastable energy state dictated by how fast it was cooled. If you take an amorphous solid thin film (e.g., made by PVD) and heat it up, it will often crystallize abruptly or behave unpredictably because it has a highly stressed, ultra-high-energy structure. A true glass, when heated, will predictably soften into a supercooled liquid once it hits its Tg."""@en ,
                                                 "While the word \"ceramic\" instantly brings to mind brittle, rigid objects like coffee mugs, brick, or technical alumina plates, a ceramic is defined purely by its chemical bonds and atomic structure, as an inorganic non-metallic substance."@en .

